  "query_preview": "..\nToday and Saturday: A 200 mb jet streak will traverse across the \nWA/OR border today, bringing a band of 150-200% of normal PWATs to \nthe southern half of WA/southern ID Panhandle. Synoptic ascent will \nbe aided by a shortwave trough embedded in the zonal flow off the WA \ncoast and upper level divergence in the left exit region of the jet. \nSnow levels will be around 4000-5500 feet, posing only a small risk \nfor snow at Stevens Pass and Lookout Pass this evening. There is a \n20% chance of least an inch of snow at Stevens Pass and a 80% chance \nLookout Pass through Friday night. Through tonight, There is a 20-\n50% chance of half an inch of rain for the Lower Columbia Basin \nmainly south of I-90, 40-80% chance for the Palouse, and 50-90% \nchance for the ID Panhandle south of I-90. There will be a fairly \nsharp gradient in the precipitation totals to the north near Spokane \nwhere PWATs are lower (nearly no precipitation expected). The Gwen \nburn scar east of Lewiston on US-12 will be vulnerable to rock \nslides this evening as soils become more saturated. Mid level \ncooling and the best lift concentrated in the dendritic layer may \nlead to a brief transition to snow on the ID Palouse and Camas \nPrairie, although accumulations will be limited with temperatures in \nthe 40s currently. There is a 60-80% chance of 1 inch of snow on the \nCamas Prairie tonight according to the REFS ensemble, although \nconfidence is low. PWATs will drop quickly this evening as as the \nshortwave brings drier air in from the northwest and precipitation \nwill shut off fairly quickly. Saturday will be dry under the \ninfluence of a shortwave ridge over the area with near normal \ntemperatures in the 30s and 40s. \n\nSunday through Friday: The surface low pressure currently 1300 miles \noff the northern CA coast will approach the northwestern US and \ninitially begin to weaken, but will restrengthen Sunday as an upper \nlevel jet and trough strengthens upstream in the north central \nPacific. Precipitation chances increase Sunday evening and overnight \nas the surface low approaches the WA/OR coast and increasing \ndiffluent southwesterly 500 mb flow overspreads the region. Snow \nlevels Sunday night into Monday will vary widely, but the best \nchances for accumulating snow will be at Stevens Pass and Washington \nPass. There is a small chance of wet snowflakes mixing into \nStehekin, Leavenworth, Plain, and Winthrop as low level max wet bulb \ntemperatures are around freezing on global models. Surface \ntemperatures and lighter precipitation rates will be limiting \nfactors if any snow does fall in the Cascade valleys Sunday night \ninto Monday. The northern valleys are in the same boat with wet snow \nchances down to the valley floors through Monday morning but with \nlimited to no accumulation. From Sunday night to Tuesday morning, \nthe chance of at least 4 inches of snow are maximized in the \nCascades around 25% for Stevens Pass and 75% chance for Washington \nPass. Snow levels will be too high for accumulating snow at Lookout \nPass as deep southwest flow advects warmer air into the area. The \nremainder of next week (Tuesday through Friday) will feature \nseasonal temperatures with periodic episodes of light snow at the \nCascade passes and Lookout Pass under mostly zonal flow. /DB",
